  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.073,
            0.135,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.187,
            0.071,
            0.495,
            0.089
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part II"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.11,
            0.883,
            0.168
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Finding a suitable counterexample is an important ingredient of mathematics because it allows us to show in a short and convincing way that a proposition is false."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.168,
            0.882,
            0.226
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Here is a famous example. Fermat claimed that all numbers of the form "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2 ^ { 2 ^ { n } } + 1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " are prime "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( n = 0 , 1 , 2 , 3 , \\ldots ) ; \\mathrm { e . g . }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , the number "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2 ^ { 2 ^ { 3 } } + 1 = 2 5 7"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . But Euler disproved this by showing that the number "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2 ^ { 2 ^ { 5 } } + 1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is composite."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.231,
            0.287,
            0.25
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 2:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.25,
            0.881,
            0.287
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Proposition P: If a number is a multiple of 6, then it is a multiple of 3."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.288,
            0.88,
            0.309
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Converse Q: If a number is a multiple of 3, then it is a multiple of 6."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.314,
            0.611,
            0.333
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Proposition P is true. Justify why this is so."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.333,
            0.882,
            0.391
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Does this now mean that all multiples of 3 are also multiples of 6? This corresponds to the converse statement Q. Determine if it is true and if not, give a counterexample."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.394,
            0.696,
            0.415
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 3:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " In this example, n is any positive integer."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.417,
            0.881,
            0.455
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Proposition P: If n is a perfect square, then it has an odd number of factors."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.455,
            0.881,
            0.495
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Converse Q: If n has an odd number of factors, then it is a perfect square."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.5,
            0.881,
            0.54
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You may recall that we came across these statements in the previous grade. Which of them are true?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.556,
            0.257,
            0.576
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Discussion"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.588,
            0.495,
            0.609
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Consider the following argument."
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.621,
            0.858,
            0.699
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Each factor of a number has a ‘partner’ factor such that their product yields the given number, e.g., 5 is a factor of 35, and 5 × 7 = 35. Here, 7 is the partner factor of 5, and vice-versa. Let us focus on factor-partner pairs of numbers, e.g., (1, 12), (2, 6), (3, 4) are the pairs for the number 12."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.702,
            0.857,
            0.778
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If a number has an odd number of factors, there must be a factor-partner pair in which the same number repeats (e.g., the partner factor of 5 in 25). If not, the given number will have an even number of factors since each factor can be paired with its factor pair."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.78,
            0.857,
            0.82
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Thus, if a number has an odd number of factors, then it is a perfect square."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.833,
            0.666,
            0.853
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What does this argument prove? Statement P or Q?"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
